WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.761
A student is watching an algebra lecture on expanding brackets.

00:00:03.811 --> 00:00:06.956
They write an answer, hesitate, erase it,

00:00:06.956 --> 00:00:10.279
and fix it: they'd forgotten the middle term.

00:00:10.495 --> 00:00:12.191
Their final notes look perfect.

00:00:12.191 --> 00:00:16.829
But every note app threw away the most important part:

00:00:16.829 --> 00:00:19.943
the mistake, and the moment they fixed it.

00:00:20.025 --> 00:00:22.932
The pauses, the erasing, the rewrites:

00:00:22.932 --> 00:00:25.934
that's where learning actually happens.

00:00:25.934 --> 00:00:29.275
And today, nobody sees it.

00:00:29.325 --> 00:00:30.783
Inkling keeps them.

00:00:33.281 --> 00:00:35.737
Inkling was built by Pranav Chowdary.

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:37.715
This is Inkling.

00:00:37.715 --> 00:00:41.083
Here it is in the app, on a real calculus lecture.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.488
Here's Maya, taking notes while the lecture plays.

00:00:44.488 --> 00:00:48.295
Every stroke is tied to that moment in the lecture.

00:00:53.176 --> 00:00:56.274
She hesitates, then erases cos(x²).

00:00:56.274 --> 00:00:58.681
Inkling keeps it as ghost ink.

00:00:58.906 --> 00:01:01.279
She rewrites it and ends the session.

00:01:02.844 --> 00:01:06.637
Inkling pairs the mistake with its fix, right on the page.

00:01:06.804 --> 00:01:10.374
Across a full lecture, Inkling marks corrected mistakes,

00:01:10.374 --> 00:01:14.755
moments she got stuck, and breakthroughs, all from how she writes.

00:01:14.805 --> 00:01:18.730
At 1:40, she rewrote cos(x²) as cos(x²) · 2x.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:24.238
Inkling reads the change: she forgot the inner derivative.

00:01:24.536 --> 00:01:28.360
It re-teaches that one step and asks a check question.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.431
She gets it right: a breakthrough.

00:01:31.666 --> 00:01:33.609
The same help comes in her own language,

00:01:33.609 --> 00:01:36.268
here Spanish, with the math unchanged.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.866
Here she got stuck while the lecturer kept talking.

00:01:40.866 --> 00:01:44.038
Replay plays the exact 20 seconds she missed.

00:01:44.278 --> 00:01:48.019
Inkling uses Signal Lab to detect where she got stuck.

00:01:48.019 --> 00:01:51.289
Every stroke is time-stamped to the lecture.

00:01:51.289 --> 00:01:52.994
Every 10 seconds,

00:01:52.994 --> 00:01:56.486
Inkling tracks four signals against her own normal:

00:01:56.486 --> 00:01:59.144
pauses while the lecturer keeps talking,

00:01:59.144 --> 00:02:01.747
slower writing, extra erasing,

00:02:01.747 --> 00:02:04.244
and pen pressure when the pen reports it.

00:02:04.244 --> 00:02:08.267
Each spike in the score becomes a moment to re-teach.

00:02:08.998 --> 00:02:12.303
A Notability export keeps only the final page.

00:02:12.303 --> 00:02:14.352
Inkling shows everything it hid,

00:02:14.352 --> 00:02:17.293
and can send it all back as a PDF.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.984
Open gaps carry into her next session,

00:02:19.984 --> 00:02:21.932
until she writes through them.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:24.234
For teachers, Inkling shows where

00:02:24.234 --> 00:02:26.446
the whole class slowed down together,

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:29.043
and the moments most worth re-teaching,

00:02:29.043 --> 00:02:30.670
all from anonymous pen data.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.624
Next.js runs the app,

00:02:32.624 --> 00:02:36.289
Gemini reads the handwriting, Groq writes the help,

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:38.095
ElevenLabs speaks it,

00:02:38.095 --> 00:02:40.792
Tiger Data stores every pen stroke as time-series,

00:02:40.792 --> 00:02:45.335
Inkling uses its own Signal Lab to detect where you got stuck,

00:02:45.335 --> 00:02:48.270
and it all round-trips with Notability.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.901
The code is on GitHub.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.272
Inkling.

00:02:52.272 --> 00:02:54.791
Every other app deletes your mistakes.

00:02:54.791 --> 00:02:56.154
We keep them.

